From: PS5_Proton <local>
Subject: [PATCH] gpu-broker: a capture is written on its own thread, not in the present

A present that carried a capture (a probe's, or a pad script's `capture`) did all of this before it returned: wait for
its flip to be shown, read the 1920x1080 frame back from the display's buffer pixel by pixel through the tiling, then
write and fsync a 6 MiB PPM. The client's frame stopped 0.63-0.75 s each time. In a Direct3D 12 game's runs every one of
the 60 ten-second windows that held a capture had that gap, and no window without one did (except at startup and the
save's load); it was read as the game's own hitch, "~690 ms every 20 s" when the scripts captured every 20 s
(PS5_Proton docs/PLAN.md; runs of 2026-10-17, record removed from the tree; in git history).

Now the present fills the same frame, pointer included, a second time into tiled memory of its own (the display copy's
own code, 8.4 MiB) and hands it to one capture thread, which writes PATH.part, fsyncs it and renames it to PATH, so the
title's pad script, which polls for PATH, never reads half a picture (PS5_Proton src/pw_display_capture.h, host-tested
in tests/test_pw_display_capture.c). It never reads the display's buffer back: that reads at about 105 MB/s, and in the
first version of this patch a whole-buffer copy from it took 80 ms in the present (2026-10-17). At most two
pictures wait; a third waits in the present for the oldest. When the copy's memory or the thread cannot be had, the
present writes the picture itself as before, once the flip is shown (the same writer, so PATH.part and the rename too).
Closing the display waits for every picture first. Each picture written is one line in the broker's log: "gpu-display
capture flip= copy_us= write_us= status= path=". The picture is what the flip shows; the present's wait for the flip to
be shown is gone. Console: in PS5_Proton runs of 2026-10-17 and 2026-10-18 (record removed from the tree; in git history), 17 captures a run,
none failed: 6.8 ms in their present (copy_us), the file written on the capture thread (write_us about 345 ms); windows
with a capture had a median worst gap of 15.5-21.6 ms, as those without.
